[{"id":93,"date_year":1982,"date_month":"MAR","date_day":22,"artist":"The Cars","venue":"Capital Centre","location":"Landover, MD, USA","details":{"lineup":["The Cars (headliner)","Nick Lowe and The Chaps (opening act)"],"setlistArtist":"The Cars","setlist":["Moving in Stereo","Since You're Gone","Dangerous Type","Touch and Go","Candy-O","A Dream Away","Shake It Up","Just What I Needed","Cruisin'","Drive","My Best Friend's Girl","Good Times Roll","You're All I've Got Tonight","Bye Bye Love"],"notes":"Part of the massive North American arena tour supporting the band's fourth album, Shake It Up. Nick Lowe, the legendary British singer-songwriter and producer, opened with The Chaps, bringing a pub-rock and rootsy old-wave energy — twanging guitars and soulful organ — as a stark contrast to the headliner's heavy synthesizers. The Cars opened with a silhouette intro: the arena lights stayed down except for three massive lighting towers, and each member walked out one by one as their instrument entered 'Moving in Stereo.' A Washington Post review of this exact show noted how faithfully the band recreated their studio wizardry live, but also how famously unanimated they were — standing completely still at their stations, barely speaking to the crowd. 'Drive' was often sung beautifully by Ben Orr."}},
{"id":92,"date_year":1983,"date_month":"APR","date_day":21,"artist":"Stray Cats","venue":"Baltimore Civic Center","location":"Baltimore, MD, USA","details":{"lineup":["Stray Cats (headliner)","Tommy Keene (opening act)"],"setlistArtist":"Stray Cats","setlist":["Baby Blue Eyes","Double Talkin' Baby","Rumble in Brixton","Stray Cat Strut","Crying Shame","Drink That Bottle Down","Rev It Up and Go","Build for Speed","Lonely Summer Nights","(She's) Sexy + 17","Runaway Boys","Rock This Town","Somethin' Else"],"notes":"Played at the peak of the trio's mainstream US popularity, on tour behind Built for Speed and staples on MTV. The sold-out crowd of 13,000 reflected the moment's rockabilly fashion subculture — pompadours, leather jackets, rockabilly style. Tommy Keene, the D.C.-area power-pop singer-songwriter, opened and later cited this show as one of the most exciting of his career. The Stray Cats defied typical arena rock setups, filling the Civic Center with just a stand-up drum kit (Slim Jim Phantom playing standing), an upright bass, and a hollow-body guitar. Coming just before the release of Rant N' Rave with the Stray Cats, Baltimore fans were among the first to hear '(She's) Sexy + 17' live before it hit radio. 'Baby Blue Eyes' was a Johnny Burnette Trio cover and 'Somethin' Else' was an Eddie Cochran cover; Lee Rocker took lead vocals on 'Drink That Bottle Down.'"}},
